-- Functions that HyperXTalk (Emily-Elizabeth Howard's fork of LiveCode
-- Community, https://github.com/emily-elizabeth/HyperXTalk, GPLv3) has as
-- functions of its engine, here as handlers of a library, so that adding
-- them breaks no script: a library's handlers come last in the message
-- path, so a script's own handlers of these names come first, as they did,
-- and a call that found no handler before now finds one here. Making them
-- functions of the engine would have reserved their names, which a
-- variable, parameter or handler could then no longer have.
--
-- HyperXTalk's "the microseconds" and its "matches wildcard" and "matches
-- regex" operators are in the engine (funcs.h, operator.h), read only where
-- what holds them did not compile without them (scriptpt.h, MCNewSyntax).
-- The work behind quotedPrintableEncode, quotedPrintableDecode and
-- microseconds() is the engine's too (engine/src/exec-filters.cpp,
-- exec-datetime.cpp): the library binds to it by name.
--
-- The handlers' names are not all lower case (Iff, microSeconds): lc-compile
-- keeps such names for syntax and warns of them, and the extension build
-- turns its warnings into errors. A script finds a library handler whatever
-- the case of the name it calls, so iff() and microseconds() reach them,
-- and the doc blocks give the names as scripts write them.

/**
Functions from HyperXTalk: <iff>, <quotedPrintableEncode>,
<quotedPrintableDecode> and <microseconds>, for scripts written for
HyperXTalk.

Description:
HyperXTalk, a fork of LiveCode Community, has these functions in its
engine. Here they are handlers of a library, which come after every other
handler in the message path: where a script has its own handler of one of
these names, that handler is called, as before.

The engine has the rest of what HyperXTalk added to the language: the
<microseconds> function (`the microseconds`) and the <matches> operator.

In a standalone application, include the "HyperXTalk Compatibility"
library (Standalone Application Settings, Inclusions).

Tags: Library
*/

library org.openxtalk.library.hxtcompat

metadata version is "1.0.0"
metadata author is "OXT-Beyond"
metadata title is "HyperXTalk Compatibility"

use com.livecode.foreign

private foreign handler MCHyperXTalkQuotedPrintableEncode(in pData as Data, \
		out rText as String) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCHyperXTalkQuotedPrintableDecode(in pText as String, \
		out rData as Data) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCHyperXTalkMicroseconds(out rMicroseconds as CDouble) \
		returns nothing binds to "<builtin>"

/**
Name: iff

Syntax: iff(<pCondition>, <pTrue>, <pFalse>)

Summary: Returns one of two values, chosen by a condition.

Example:
put iff(tCount is 1, "item", "items") into tWord

Example:
answer "The file is" && iff(there is a file tPath, "there", "missing") & "."

Parameters:
pCondition: An expression that is true or false.
pTrue: The value to return when <pCondition> is true.
pFalse: The value to return when <pCondition> is false.

Returns: <pTrue> if <pCondition> is true, <pFalse> if it is false.

Description:
Use the <iff> function to choose between two values in an expression,
where an <if> statement would take several lines.

As with any handler, both values are worked out before <iff> is called,
so a value that can fail, such as `1 / tCount` when <tCount> can be 0,
fails even when it is not the one returned: use an <if> statement for
it. (HyperXTalk's <iff> works out only the value it returns.)

If <pCondition> is neither true nor false, the call fails with
"call: type conversion error".

<iff> comes from HyperXTalk. Where a script has its own <iff> handler,
that handler is called instead.

Tags: Library

References: if (control structure), true (constant), false (constant)
*/
public handler Iff(in pCondition as Boolean, in pTrue as optional any, \
		in pFalse as optional any) returns optional any
	if pCondition then
		return pTrue
	end if
	return pFalse
end handler

/**
Summary: Encodes data as quoted-printable text, as email does.

Example:
put quotedPrintableEncode(textEncode(field "Message", "UTF-8")) into tBody

Parameters:
pData: The data to encode.

Returns: The quoted-printable text.

Description:
Use the <quotedPrintableEncode> function to encode text or data for an
email message whose Content-Transfer-Encoding is quoted-printable (RFC
2045).

Bytes from 33 to 126 stay as they are, but "=", and so do spaces and
tabs, unless one ends a line. Line breaks (CR and LF) stay as they are.
Every other byte becomes "=" and its two hexadecimal digits, upper case.
A longer line is broken with soft line breaks ("=" at the end of a line,
then CR LF), so that no line is longer than 76 characters.

Text with characters outside the ASCII range should be encoded first,
with <textEncode> (for example in UTF-8), and the message should say
which encoding it is in.

<quotedPrintableEncode> comes from HyperXTalk. HyperXTalk's can make a
line of 77 characters, which the RFC does not allow. Where a script has
its own <quotedPrintableEncode> handler, that handler is called instead.

Tags: Library

References: quotedPrintableDecode (handler), textEncode (function),
base64Encode (function)
*/
public handler quotedPrintableEncode(in pData as Data) returns String
	variable tText as String
	variable tDone as Boolean
	unsafe
		put MCHyperXTalkQuotedPrintableEncode(pData, tText) into tDone
	end unsafe
	if not tDone then
		throw "quotedPrintableEncode: out of memory"
	end if
	return tText
end handler

/**
Summary: Decodes quoted-printable text, as email has it.

Example:
put textDecode(quotedPrintableDecode(tBody), "UTF-8") into field "Message"

Parameters:
pText: The quoted-printable text to decode.

Returns: The data it encodes.

Description:
Use the <quotedPrintableDecode> function to decode the body of an email
message whose Content-Transfer-Encoding is quoted-printable (RFC 2045).

An "=" and two hexadecimal digits (in upper or lower case) become the
byte they encode. A soft line break ("=" at the end of a line) is left
out, with its line break. Everything else stays as it is, an "=" that
starts neither included.

The result is data: decode it as text with <textDecode>, in the
encoding the message names.

<quotedPrintableDecode> comes from HyperXTalk. Where a script has its own
<quotedPrintableDecode> handler, that handler is called instead.

Tags: Library

References: quotedPrintableEncode (handler), textDecode (function),
base64Decode (function)
*/
public handler quotedPrintableDecode(in pText as String) returns Data
	variable tData as Data
	variable tDone as Boolean
	unsafe
		put MCHyperXTalkQuotedPrintableDecode(pText, tData) into tDone
	end unsafe
	if not tDone then
		throw "quotedPrintableDecode: out of memory"
	end if
	return tData
end handler

/**
Name: microseconds

Syntax: microseconds()

Summary: Returns the number of microseconds since midnight GMT, January
1, 1970, when it is called with brackets.

Example:
put microseconds() into tStart

Returns: The number of microseconds since the start of the eon, a whole
number.

Description:
`microseconds()` is the <microseconds> function (`the microseconds`),
which the engine has, written as HyperXTalk also allows: with brackets,
as a call of a handler. Where a script has its own <microseconds>
function handler, `microseconds()` calls that handler, as before, and
`the microseconds` is still the engine's.

Tags: Library

References: microseconds (function), milliseconds (function)
*/
public handler microSeconds() returns Number
	variable tMicroseconds as Number
	unsafe
		MCHyperXTalkMicroseconds(tMicroseconds)
	end unsafe
	return tMicroseconds
end handler

/**
Name: microsecs

Syntax: microsecs()

Summary: The same as <microseconds>.

Example:
put microsecs() into tStart

Returns: The number of microseconds since midnight GMT, January 1, 1970.

Description:
`microsecs()` is `microseconds()`: another of its names.

Tags: Library

References: microseconds (handler)
*/
public handler microSecs() returns Number
	return microSeconds()
end handler

/**
Name: microsecond

Syntax: microsecond()

Summary: The same as <microseconds>.

Example:
put microsecond() into tStart

Returns: The number of microseconds since midnight GMT, January 1, 1970.

Description:
`microsecond()` is `microseconds()`: another of its names.

Tags: Library

References: microseconds (handler)
*/
public handler microSecond() returns Number
	return microSeconds()
end handler

/**
Name: microsec

Syntax: microsec()

Summary: The same as <microseconds>.

Example:
put microsec() into tStart

Returns: The number of microseconds since midnight GMT, January 1, 1970.

Description:
`microsec()` is `microseconds()`: another of its names.

Tags: Library

References: microseconds (handler)
*/
public handler microSec() returns Number
	return microSeconds()
end handler

end library
